      "text": "What you’re describing is usually less a “pricing” problem than a combination of pricing strategy, positioning, and sales/objection handling. In LA, I’d start with someone who can look at all three rather than a generic business coach.\n\nA few options:\n\n- LA Regional SBDC — probably the best first stop. They provide no-cost, confidential one-on-one business advising throughout Los Angeles and specifically work on areas including sales and pricing. ([smallbizla.org](https://smallbizla.org/?utm_source=openai))\n- SCORE Los Angeles — also free. Their mentor directory lets you specifically filter for “Pricing” and “Sales,” so you can find someone whose background fits a service business. ([score.org](https://www.score.org/losangeles/mentors/?utm_source=openai))\n- Rachel Yacobucci — a private strategic business/pricing coach specifically focused on service-based business owners. Potentially a closer fit if you want hands-on help restructuring how you package and price your services. ([rachelyacobucci.com](https://www.rachelyacobucci.com/?utm_source=openai))\n- Outcomes Not Ideas — focuses specifically on sales coaching for service businesses, including value-based pricing and handling price objections. This could be useful if your prices are basically sound but customers repeatedly negotiate you down during the sales conversation. ([outcomesnotideas.com](https://www.outcomesnotideas.com/?utm_source=openai))\n\nBefore paying a consultant, I’d book an LA SBDC advisor and tell them: “Customers regularly ask me to lower my quoted price. I want to determine whether I have a pricing, positioning, lead-quality, or sales-process problem.”\n\nIf you tell me what kind of service business you run (e.g. cleaning, landscaping, photography, consulting, construction), roughly what you charge, and whether your customers are consumers or businesses, I can narrow this to 2–3 LA people/resources that are particularly appropriate."
